+# Epic 2 Context: Packet Types & QoS
+
+<!-- Compiled from planning artifacts. Edit freely. Regenerate with compile-epic-context if planning docs change. -->
+
+## Goal
+
+Deliver the game's core differentiator: packets have needs (class roster), and
+the player engineers link-level QoS. Each pipe carries 3 DiffServ-style priority
+lanes (Express/Standard/Best-effort); the player allocates the pipe's bandwidth
+across lanes (WFQ-style integer weights) via a single "priority emphasis" dial
+in the MVP. The player promotes/demotes per type (all traffic starts Standard);
+the player owns every call — nothing auto-allocates. QoS is bandwidth-on-a-pipe
+(lane scheduling inside a link), strictly orthogonal to routing (next-hop
+selection); a bundled parallel-pipe link pools capacity and carries the same 3
+lanes.
+
+## Stories
+
+- Story 3.1: Packet types + the demand director (weighted-random spawns) — done.
+- Story 3.2: 3-lane QoS + emphasis dial (per-pipe weight presets; allocation).
+- Story 3.3: Node serialization + contention drop ladder (work-conserving, WRR floor).
+- Story 3.4: Per-class SLA accumulators inside Flow.
+
+## Requirements & Constraints
+
+- Each pipe carries exactly 3 lanes; bandwidth partitions by integer WFQ weights
+  via largest-remainder distribution with Express→Standard→Best tie order.
+- Allocation is weight-only: demand is NOT an input.
+- All traffic starts on Standard (the neutral middle); the player promotes what
+  can't drop/lag and demotes what can wait; a type's lane can be overridden
+  per-pipe.
+- MVP exposes the allocation as a single "priority emphasis" dial per pipe
+  mapping to weight presets; full per-lane raw weights are a depth layer.
+- Contention consequence (per-class loss/latency) and serialization are later
+  stories; 3.2 makes the allocation itself visible.
+- Edge contracts: all-zero weights → catalog default preset (E5); a never-drop
+  class lane is floored to a quantum and a zeroing edit is rejected (E6);
+  largest-remainder distribution pinned (E8).
+
+## Technical Decisions
+
+- QoS is NOT a system: pure procs (`qos_allocate`, later `qos_serialize`) in
+  package core's flow files, invoked by `flow_step` [ODN-3]. Lane weights /
+  policies are Topology-owned data.
+- Determinism spine (ODN-1/9/10/11): integer-only math (sums in i64), arrays
+  only (no maps), owned seeded RNG, per-tick T1 state hashes, action-log replay
+  gate with catalog_hash/logic_hz binding. New commands append union variants +
+  wire tags (CMD_TAG_*); LOG_VERSION bumps only on incompatible record shape.
+- Data-driven catalogs (ODN-5): JSON single source in data/*.json, fail-fast
+  validated at load, read-only after. Sim values are ints. balance.json is the
+  tunable-numbers home.
+- Edit authority: core's validate-then-apply is the single legality path (live
+  fast-path + replay converge); typed Edit_Error rejections (never strings).
+- Derived state (routing table, bundles) is rebuilt on Topology.gen change,
+  never serialized — the serialized form is the T1 hash input.
+- Golden verification: T1 hash manifests + T2 pixel goldens via the software
+  raylib shadow (tools/harness.sh); demos are scripted .dem files; drift-check
+  proves the replay gate rejects mutated logs.
+
+## UX & Interaction Patterns
+
+- Mouse-only graybox: drag node→node to draw (1/2/3 picks tier), right-click a
+  pipe cycles its QoS priority (prototype language), click a pipe to inspect.
+- Readability: the pipe shows the allocation (the decision); the node shows
+  serialization (the result, story 3.3). Flow within a pipe shows colored
+  packet dots whose proportion reflects the lane allocation. Color is never the
+  sole encoding — shape + icon reinforce ([UX-DR4]).
+
+## Cross-Story Dependencies
+
+- 3.2 depends on 3.1 (packet roster with default_lane, demand director) and
+  the 2.x spine (draw/demolish, bundles, ECMP).
+- 3.3 consumes 3.2's weights/allocator for serialization + drops.
+- 3.4 consumes the flow's delivery/drop path for SLA accumulators.
+- Placement (story 3.5) is IN FLIGHT on origin/v2 — QoS work must not touch
+  placement files (Cmd_Place_Router, tray).
